## Context

The relevance filter (flag 6) carries a measured language bias. Evidence, all
on corpus v2, logged in 0337 and 0283 on 2026-07-28:

- Removal ablation: non-English works removed at 41.8% vs English 19.9%.
  Flag 6 carries 58.9% of non-English removals vs 50.4% of English ones — the
  only flag skewed that way. Abstract coverage is ruled out as confound
  (non-English coverage is higher, 88.0% vs 85.7%).
- Paired-query experiment (1,589 non-English works, GPU, seed 20260728):
  scoring against an own-language translation of the deployed English query
  lifts pass rate 55.3% → 66.8% (+11.5pp, Wilcoxon p=3.2e-104). Symmetric
  control: 600 English works scored against foreign queries lose 6.5–48.7pp.
  The cross-encoder partly measures query–document language match, not
  relevance. Model is BAAI/bge-reranker-v2-m3 (already multilingual); the
  monolingual element is the query string.
- The 0.818 human validation (0372) is de facto an English-stratum
  validation: 97 of its 100 works are English.

## The design (author, 2026-07-28)

Two-tier flag 6:

- **English (~39.5k works)**: cross-encoder + 0.002 threshold, unchanged —
  validated at AUC 0.818 on what is now visibly its own stratum.
- **Non-English + unknown (3,642 works)**: relevance adjudicated by a
  3-seat AI panel (Fable 5, GPT-5.6, Kimi 3 — three vendors, decorrelated),
  majority vote, judged in-language, blinded to reranker scores. Protocol in
  0541.
- Other flags, protection rules, and the score column unchanged. Bounded
  change: the diff to refined_works is confined to the non-EN stratum.

## Why not panel the whole 43,179? (author asked; answer before Go)

Cost is NOT the discriminator. Measured token volumes (title[:300] +
abstract[:1500], 250-token prompt overhead, 80-token output, 3 seats):

| scope | works | calls | $ budget | $ mid | $ premium |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| bridge (batch-2 anchor) | 94 | 282 | 0.15 | 0.49 | 0.99 |
| non-EN flag-6-flagged only | 949 | 2,847 | 1.47 | 4.84 | 9.68 |
| non-EN + unknown | 3,642 | 10,926 | 5.52 | 18.11 | 36.23 |
| full corpus | 43,179 | 129,537 | 65.94 | 216.33 | 432.66 |

Price points: budget $0.6/$2.5 per M in/out, mid $2/$8, premium $4/$16 —
confirm live prices at run time. Even full-corpus at premium is ~$430.

The real arguments:

- **For full corpus**: one uniform instrument kills the asymmetric-standards
  objection entirely; simplest §2.3; retires the cross-encoder.
- **Against**: it re-adjudicates the one stratum whose instrument is
  *validated against human labels* (0.818/81% on the English sample) —
  replacing a measured instrument where it is not broken; blast radius
  becomes corpus-wide (every count's provenance changes, not just the
  non-EN stratum); 130k calls of wall-clock and rate-limit exposure; and the
  reproducibility story for a data paper weakens (local model → API panel)
  across the whole corpus instead of one stratum.
- **The bridge decides it**: 0541 phase A grades the 94 human-labelled
  batch-2 works first. If the panel clearly dominates the cross-encoder on
  English hard cases (κ vs human), full-corpus becomes the honest choice;
  if they are comparable, two-tier is the defensible minimum change.

